index-readiness: not established: the response exposes no total-match figure, so this capture cannot say whether the index was populated (search engine reached directly: no, observed 000)
page-window: fixed by the request URL above and therefore identical in the
  replay; the engine returns one page, so hits-listed-below may be smaller
  than total-match-count without anything having been dropped here
ordering-treatment: as returned.  Not sorted, not deduplicated, not
  reordered, not truncated by this transcription, and no score removed.
  Ordering is behaviour, not presentation.
query-time-figure: removed by the normaliser and shown as <QUERY-TIME-MS>
  in the body above.  It is the ONLY value inside a search response this
  capture removes, because it measures how long the engine spent rather
  than what the engine returned; the count, the identifiers, their order
  and the scores are all left exactly as received.
score-absence-note: the request URL above does not ask the engine for a
  relevance score, so none is exposed per hit and none has been dropped.
  The RETURNED ORDER below is then the whole of the ranking evidence,
  which is why it is transcribed verbatim.
